WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.201 --> 00:00:02.435
-My next guest is
an Emmy Award-winning

00:00:02.435 --> 00:00:06.205
actor, author, performer,
and, as of yesterday,

00:00:06.205 --> 00:00:08.408
he is now also
officially an astronaut.

00:00:08.408 --> 00:00:09.742
[ Drumroll ]

00:00:09.742 --> 00:00:13.279
Back from his trip to
outer space with Blue Origin,

00:00:13.279 --> 00:00:16.449
please welcome the one-and-only
William Shatner!

00:00:16.449 --> 00:00:17.483
[ Cheers and applause ]
♪♪

00:00:17.483 --> 00:00:19.585
That's what I'm talking about!

00:00:19.585 --> 00:00:21.588
Hey!

00:00:21.588 --> 00:00:23.156
Oh, my gosh.

00:00:23.156 --> 00:00:25.591
I'm --
-Oh, my God. An astronaut.

00:00:25.591 --> 00:00:27.059
We're not really.

00:00:27.059 --> 00:00:30.496
You know, astronauts really --
they go up and they do things.

00:00:30.496 --> 00:00:32.799
We're half astronauts.

00:00:32.799 --> 00:00:36.235
-No, no, no. Half astronauts.

00:00:36.235 --> 00:00:39.238
Astronauts. Thank you.
Yeah.

00:00:39.238 --> 00:00:40.973
William,
I'm so happy to see you.

00:00:40.973 --> 00:00:43.008
And I really wanted to
talk to you.

00:00:43.008 --> 00:00:46.012
This is so exciting for me,
because I loved it so much.

00:00:46.012 --> 00:00:47.747
And I'll tell you why
I loved it.

00:00:47.747 --> 00:00:49.949
One is because, obviously,
you spent a majority of

00:00:49.949 --> 00:00:53.653
your life talking about space
and, you know, "Star Trek,"

00:00:53.653 --> 00:00:54.887
and you're Captain Kirk.

00:00:54.887 --> 00:00:57.256
Here's why I really loved it.
You come down.

00:00:57.256 --> 00:00:59.025
You land in this desert thing.

00:00:59.025 --> 00:01:00.926
You come out of the capsule.

00:01:00.926 --> 00:01:02.528
Jeff Bezos comes over.

00:01:02.528 --> 00:01:05.465
Then he comes in with champagne,
and he's like, "Whoo-hoo!"

00:01:05.465 --> 00:01:07.533
And you go, "No.

00:01:07.533 --> 00:01:08.935
Hey. No.

00:01:08.935 --> 00:01:11.604
Abs-- No, not now, buddy.
Absolutely not.

00:01:11.604 --> 00:01:12.805
I don't want the champagne.

00:01:12.805 --> 00:01:15.307
I've got to think about
what just happened."

00:01:15.307 --> 00:01:20.146
-Well, you know, the whole
effect of going into space

00:01:20.146 --> 00:01:24.316
and seeing what
very few people have seen,

00:01:24.316 --> 00:01:25.651
and there is
no frame of reference.

00:01:25.651 --> 00:01:28.120
How do you
describe weightlessness?

00:01:28.120 --> 00:01:32.258
How do you describe seeing
palpable darkness,

00:01:32.258 --> 00:01:35.795
blackness of space,
where there is no filter of

00:01:35.795 --> 00:01:38.965
dust or dirt or
reflections of light?

00:01:38.965 --> 00:01:41.568
It's there.
It's like hanging there.

00:01:41.568 --> 00:01:45.338
It's evil.
It seems like the monster.

00:01:45.338 --> 00:01:49.375
And then you look down
and you see this precious thing,

00:01:49.375 --> 00:01:52.946
this warm, nurturing Earth.

00:01:52.946 --> 00:01:55.848
And you see death and life.

00:01:55.848 --> 00:01:58.851
And you descend
and then you're overwhelmed by

00:01:58.851 --> 00:02:01.787
the possibility of the Earth
ending in a short while.

00:02:01.787 --> 00:02:03.889
And your life is
in front of you.

00:02:03.889 --> 00:02:05.325
But the message is interesting.

00:02:05.325 --> 00:02:09.161
The message is,
we all need a wake-up call

00:02:09.161 --> 00:02:11.831
every so often in our lives
where your life

00:02:11.831 --> 00:02:14.234
is threatened by something.

00:02:14.234 --> 00:02:19.304
You may have a near accident
or somebody you love has died.

00:02:19.304 --> 00:02:20.572
And, all of a sudden, you look
at your life and you say,

00:02:20.572 --> 00:02:21.574
"Wait a minute.

00:02:21.574 --> 00:02:23.208
What's important here?"

00:02:23.208 --> 00:02:26.846
And you have that wake-up call
and you need it every so often,

00:02:26.846 --> 00:02:29.782
because then, you know, coming
and telling jokes

00:02:29.782 --> 00:02:32.518
is very important, and you lose
sight of the fact that,

00:02:32.518 --> 00:02:34.153
yes, that's important,
but what is

00:02:34.153 --> 00:02:39.492
equally and perhaps more
important is caring, loving.

00:02:39.492 --> 00:02:42.195
The planet is
going to be inundated

00:02:42.195 --> 00:02:43.529
unless we do something about it.

00:02:43.529 --> 00:02:48.167
I mean, really bad, and we got
to do something about it.

00:02:48.167 --> 00:02:49.602
And what are
we going to do about it?

00:02:49.602 --> 00:02:51.070
And what am I going to do
with my life?

00:02:51.070 --> 00:02:55.407
And so all the deep things that
we should be thinking about,

00:02:55.407 --> 00:02:58.211
every so often,
we need to be reminded.

00:02:58.211 --> 00:03:03.349
And that moon shot,
that thing -- that did it to me.

00:03:03.349 --> 00:03:06.952
And I was overwhelmed by
all the things

00:03:06.952 --> 00:03:10.156
we need to do
and the loves and the losses.

00:03:10.156 --> 00:03:15.361
It was an enormous moment for me
that I never expected.

00:03:15.361 --> 00:03:17.864
I, too, have an album called
"Bill" that out's there,

00:03:17.864 --> 00:03:21.300
and there is a song on there,
"So Far From the Moon,"

00:03:21.300 --> 00:03:23.836
a time in my life when they
walked on the moon,

00:03:23.836 --> 00:03:25.671
and I was so far from it.

00:03:25.671 --> 00:03:29.542
And here I was, 50 years later,
so close to the moon.

00:03:29.542 --> 00:03:33.278
And it was
very meaningful to me.

00:03:33.278 --> 00:03:35.415
-The album, by the way,
which I just listened to --

00:03:35.415 --> 00:03:37.716
it's getting great reviews,
and I love it.

00:03:37.716 --> 00:03:39.685
I go, "Wow, William Shatner
has a new record."

00:03:39.685 --> 00:03:41.955
It is getting great reviews.
I loved it.

00:03:41.955 --> 00:03:43.889
I know you that were saying,

00:03:43.889 --> 00:03:45.858
in interviews before
you did this trip,

00:03:45.858 --> 00:03:49.062
that you going to write
a song about this.

00:03:49.062 --> 00:03:50.829
Are you still
going to write a song?

00:03:50.829 --> 00:03:51.864
And has the song changed?

00:03:51.864 --> 00:03:53.699
-Thank you, Jimmy.

00:03:53.699 --> 00:03:57.970
The other part of that,
the other astonishing part,

00:03:57.970 --> 00:04:03.676
was that Dan Miller and
"Rob the Poet" and I

00:04:03.676 --> 00:04:05.644
have worked on this album,
and it's a beautiful album.

00:04:05.644 --> 00:04:06.979
It's a work of love.

00:04:06.979 --> 00:04:11.116
And, as you said, it is getting
astonishing reviews.

00:04:11.116 --> 00:04:13.619
We met in New York a few days
ago and said, "You know,

00:04:13.619 --> 00:04:15.387
we've got to write a song
about Bill

00:04:15.387 --> 00:04:16.389
going up into space.

00:04:16.389 --> 00:04:17.390
And what's the song about?"

00:04:17.390 --> 00:04:18.491
And we started talking about

00:04:18.491 --> 00:04:19.758
what it's going to be
like, you know,

00:04:19.758 --> 00:04:23.229
the blue orb
and the thing you see.

00:04:23.229 --> 00:04:25.831
I called Rob the Poet
when I came down

00:04:25.831 --> 00:04:29.435
and I said, "Toss out
everything we talked about.

00:04:29.435 --> 00:04:31.470
Going up there
and having that experience

00:04:31.470 --> 00:04:34.440
is nothing like
what you hear about."

00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:36.042
-I just want to say also,
on behalf --

00:04:36.042 --> 00:04:38.878
I just -- I thought it
was great that you did it.

00:04:38.878 --> 00:04:41.313
I think it was also --
maybe also a wake-up call

00:04:41.313 --> 00:04:43.583
for all of us to just,
when you get a chance,

00:04:43.583 --> 00:04:45.517
just tell someone you love them.

00:04:45.517 --> 00:04:47.620
So -- Yeah.
-And that's the --

00:04:47.620 --> 00:04:49.956
-We love you, buddy.
William Shatner, everybody!

00:04:49.956 --> 00:04:50.990
[ Cheers and applause ]
-Thank you.

00:04:50.990 --> 00:04:53.259
-He went to space!

00:04:53.259 --> 00:04:54.826
He's an astronaut.
He's returned.

00:04:54.826 --> 00:04:55.928
It's William Shatner!

